;; TOOL-RFC-005, the verified core of the prose path-citation lint.
;;
;; The RFC is docs/design/tool-rfc-005-doc-path-lint.md. This module is section
;; 7's THIRD instrument: a contract that references neither implementation and
;; therefore outlives the reference script, which section 8 deletes.
;;
;; WHY THIS IS ITS OWN MODULE AND NOT PART OF THE PORT. A `def` cannot call a
;; sibling `def` in the same module: the failure is at check time, before
;; verification, and no ordering escapes it. Cross-module calls to an
;; already-verified callee do work, leaf-first. So a proved core's module split
;; is forced by the language rather than chosen for tidiness, and the port
;; imports this. Same reason as tools/doc-archive/adjudicate.llmll.
;;
;; WHAT IS PROVABLE HERE AND WHAT IS NOT. The gate has two halves and only one
;; of them is contractable today. The RECOGNIZER half decides which bytes are a
;; citation and whether a line carries a history marker; every one of those is a
;; bool-valued body over string comparison, which falls to erBodyFallback
;; (STRLIT-BODY-1), so it carries no proof. Measured, not assumed: a
;; `string-contains` recognizer and a `regex-match` recognizer BOTH report
;; `body-fallback`, while a control over ints reports `body-faithful`. That
;; measurement is what settled RFC section 9 D3, since the verified tier the
;; hand-rolled precedent was protecting is not on offer either way.
;;
;; The ADJUDICATOR half is this file. Once the six pieces of evidence are in
;; hand, "does this citation get reported" is a pure boolean function of them,
;; and boolean connectives are in the QF-LIA core, so it verifies body-faithful.
;;
;; THE CONTRACT IS AN EQUALITY, NOT AN IMPLICATION, AND THAT IS THE POINT. A
;; post of the form (=> ... result) would be satisfied by a body that reports
;; everything, and a post of (=> result ...) by one that reports nothing. Only
;; the equality pins both directions, which is what makes the refuting case
;; below bite.
;;
;; MEASURED, not assumed: this module verifies SAFE and reports
;; `body-faithful: reports?`, so the pass is not a fallback. Dropping a single
;; suppressor from the body (the `hist` disjunct) is REFUTED at constraint #0.
;; That refuting case is what separates a proof from a green light: a fallback
;; body cannot be refuted, and a postcondition of `false` on one still reports
;; SAFE.
;;
;; `or` is binary here, not variadic, so the disjunction nests.

(module adjudicate

  ;; The six pieces of evidence the scanner collects for one citation, in the
  ;; reference's own short-circuit order (doc_path_lint.py:153-161):
  ;;
  ;;   root   S1  the path resolves from the repository root
  ;;   rel    S2  it resolves relative to the directory of the citing file
  ;;   label  S3  it appears as a link LABEL whose target resolves
  ;;   ph     S4  it matches PLACEHOLDER: a pattern, not a citation
  ;;   allow  S5  the (file, path) pair is in the ALLOW table
  ;;   hist   S6  the citing line carries a historical marker
  ;;
  ;; A citation is REPORTED exactly when none of the six fired. The reference
  ;; chains S1 to S5 with `or` and tests S6 separately, but the two forms agree
  ;; on every input: `or` is commutative and neither branch has an effect. The
  ;; separation there is about which LINE is consulted, which is the scanner's
  ;; problem and not this function's.
  (def reports? [root: bool rel: bool label: bool ph: bool allow: bool hist: bool] -> bool
    (post (= result (not (or (or (or root rel) (or label ph)) (or allow hist)))))
    (not (or (or (or root rel) (or label ph)) (or allow hist))))

  ;; The finding count is what section 7's row 2 asserts against the live
  ;; corpus, and the assertion is `= 0` rather than a pinned citation total,
  ;; which would redden on the next document anyone writes.
  ;;
  ;; The contract states the two properties that make the count meaningful: it
  ;; never goes negative, and it advances by exactly one per reported citation.
  ;; A body that dropped findings on the floor would satisfy neither.
  (def tally [seen: int report: bool] -> int
    (pre  (>= seen 0))
    (post (and (>= result 0)
               (and (=> report      (= result (+ seen 1)))
                    (=> (not report) (= result seen)))))
    (if report (+ seen 1) seen))

  ;; The exit status, and the whole of what this advisory gate "decides".
  ;;
  ;; IT LIVES HERE RATHER THAN IN THE PORT FOR TWO REASONS. A `:status`
  ;; function is a strict-core `def`, so it may call only body-faithful
  ;; functions, and every accessor in the port is a `def-shell` reading Json;
  ;; PROC-BOUNDARY-1 records the same shape, that a def projecting a component
  ;; out of a composite state falls back. Taking the two integers as parameters
  ;; keeps the range obligation PROVED instead of asserted.
  ;;
  ;; The range refinement is what stops a silent success: POSIX truncates to the
  ;; low 8 bits, so an unchecked 256 would exit 0 and report success. The two
  ;; implications pin the policy itself, not merely the range: exit 1 exactly
  ;; when strict was asked for AND something was found, and 0 in every other
  ;; case. A body that failed closed would satisfy the range and be refuted by
  ;; the second implication, which is the point of writing both.
  (def status-of [strict: int found: int] -> int
    (pre  (and (>= strict 0) (>= found 0)))
    (post (and (and (>= result 0) (<= result 255))
               (and (=> (and (= strict 1) (> found 0)) (= result 1))
                    (=> (or (not (= strict 1)) (= found 0)) (= result 0)))))
    (if (and (= strict 1) (> found 0)) 1 0)))
